 "nodes": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "CA3 PATTERN COMPLETION. A recurrent excitatory population with strong internal connectivity and global inhibition, the classic autoassociative memory circuit. A cue to one end of the band recruits the rest of the population through the recurrent collaterals.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": ""
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 190,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "Watch the raster: the cue drives a few hundred cells, and within about a hundred milliseconds the rest of the band joins them, then the whole thing settles back to its spontaneous rate. Measured 2026-09-15 at density: baseline 0.4 Hz, and in the 100 ms after the cue the cued end reads 38 Hz and the far end, which the cue never touches, 24 Hz. CA3 pyramidal cells fire under 1 Hz median in an awake animal (Mizuseki and Buzsaki 2013) and reach tens of Hz inside a place field; the event rates are in that range, the silent baseline is below it.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": "readout"
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 3,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 430,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "Three things make the recall an event rather than a runaway: an absolute refractory period, short-term depression, and enough inhibition. Depression is what makes it robust rather than tuned: with it the response moves 14 percent while the recurrent weight moves 64.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": "recurrent wiring"
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
